### What is ArchUnit?

ArchUnit loads compiled classes and gives a fluent API to express structural rules as JUnit tests. A violation fails the build exactly like a failing test.

### How do you enforce layered architecture?

```java
package com.bank.archtest;

import com.tngtech.archunit.junit.AnalyzeClasses;
import com.tngtech.archunit.junit.ArchTest;
import com.tngtech.archunit.lang.ArchRule;
import static com.tngtech.archunit.library.Architectures.layeredArchitecture;

@AnalyzeClasses(packages = "com.bank")
public class LayerRulesTest {

    @ArchTest
    static final ArchRule layers = layeredArchitecture()
        .consideringAllDependencies()
        .layer("Controller").definedBy("..controller..")
        .layer("Service").definedBy("..service..")
        .layer("Repository").definedBy("..repository..")
        .whereLayer("Repository").mayOnlyBeAccessedByLayers("Service")
        .whereLayer("Service").mayOnlyBeAccessedByLayers("Controller");
}
```

```text
Expected output on violation:
java.lang.AssertionError:
Architecture Violation: Layer 'Repository' is accessed by layer 'Controller'
  com.bank.controller.TransferController -> com.bank.repository.AccountRepository
```

### How do you forbid controllers calling repositories?

```java
import static com.tngtech.archunit.lang.syntax.ArchRuleDefinition.noClasses;

@ArchTest
static final ArchRule controllersDoNotTouchRepos = noClasses()
    .that().resideInAPackage("..controller..")
    .should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAPackage("..repository..");
```

### How do you forbid sync calls inside @Transactional?

A custom ArchCondition inspects method call targets.

```java
@ArchTest
static final ArchRule noRemoteCallsInTransactions = methods()
    .that().areAnnotatedWith(Transactional.class)
    .should(notCallRemoteClients());

// Custom condition checks if any call target resides in ..client.. package
```

### How do you forbid dual writes without an outbox?

```java
@ArchTest
static final ArchRule noDualWrites = noClasses()
    .that().areNotAssignableTo(OutboxRelay.class)
    .should(dependOnBothRepositoryAndBroker());
// Flags any class using both JpaRepository AND KafkaTemplate
```

### How do you adopt rules on a large codebase?

Use `FreezingArchRule` to record current violations as a baseline. Only NEW violations fail.

```java
import com.tngtech.archunit.library.freeze.FreezingArchRule;

@ArchTest
static final ArchRule frozenLayers = FreezingArchRule.freeze(layers);
// First run: records 47 existing violations as baseline
// Next runs: only NEW violations fail the build
```

### Why does this prevent architectural drift?

Humans forget, rotate, and shortcut under deadline pressure. Code review catches structural violations inconsistently. An executable rule applies identically to every change by every author forever, documents the intent precisely, and fails fast at the cheapest moment (before merge).

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: relying on a wiki page to enforce architecture
// "Controllers must not call repositories" → forgotten within a month

// CORRECT: ArchUnit test in CI → build fails on first violation
// The rule IS the specification, machine-enforced
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Adopt rules incrementally with FreezingArchRule; do not block on a giant upfront cleanup.
- ArchUnit tests run as normal JUnit tests: `mvn test` or `gradle test`.
- Naming conventions (every @Service class, every final field) are also enforceable.
- The failure message names the exact offending class and dependency.
- Over a year across many engineers, this is the difference between a clean system and an accidental big ball of mud.
